  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 252 PM MDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nNo major changes to the overall long term forecast as we see\nconditions slightly warmer than average to start the period and\nthen by the end of the week into next weekend we warm back up to\nseveral degrees above average with monsoonal moisture providing\na daily chance of thunderstorms with a low end severe risk\ngenerally expected each day. At the upper levels, a strong ridge\nof high pressure will control the Western US, and should\ncontinue to hold control until the end of the week when a deep\nlow will approach the western Canadian coastline. This feature\nmay begin to move inland and could interrupt our pattern by late\nnext weekend, but until then expect largely similar conditions\neach day. Moisture at the surface may begin to slowly dwindle,\nand by the late week into the weekend elevated fire weather\nconcerns may begin to arise as minimum daytime RH values drop\nback into the teens. Still, daily PWAT values will sit around\n120-130% of normal, and between daytime instability and weak\ndisturbances riding the periphery of the ridge, we should still\nsee thunderstorm chances each afternoon with a low end severe\nthreat possible. At the surface meanwhile, a cold front will\nstall out on Tuesday and then begin advancing back eastwards as\na warm front but should become very slow moving to even\nstationary, helping to fuel storm initiation through the end of\nthe week. Overall it will be more of the same as we remain in a\nfairly stagnant pattern through the period.",
